/**
	A microphone records audio from an input device such as a microphone or a line input.
	microphone(int device = -1, audio_engine@ engine = sound_default_engine);
	## Arguments:
		* int device = -1: the index of the device in `sound_input_devices` to record from, or -1 for the system's default.
		* audio_engine@ engine = sound_default_engine: the engine to create the microphone in, whose sample rate and number of channels the recording uses.
	## Remarks:
		A microphone is an audio_ring_buffer, and has all of its methods and properties, and those of audio_data_source and audio_node, as well as the ones listed here. Recording starts as soon as it is created, and an exception is thrown if the device can't be opened.
		The recorded audio is kept in the buffer until it is read with `read()`, so read regularly or older audio is lost once the buffer is full. Alternatively attach the microphone's output to a node, for example an audio_encoder to save the recording to a file, or give it to `sound::open()` to hear it. Stopping the microphone with `set_state(AUDIO_NODE_STATE_STOPPED)` stops recording, and starting it again empties the buffer first.
		Recording may need permission from the user on some platforms.
*/

// Example:
void main() {
	microphone mic;
	audio_wav_encoder recording;
	recording.open("recording.wav", sound_default_engine.sample_rate, sound_default_engine.channels);
	wait(100);
	timer t;
	while (t.elapsed < 5000) { // Record for five seconds.
		recording.write(mic.read());
		wait(20);
	}
	recording.close();
	alert("Done", "Five seconds were recorded to recording.wav.");
}
